.TH DCC-CPU-DAEMON 8 2026-11-27 "Dudes Control Center @VERSION@" "System Administration"
.nh
.SH NAME
dcc-cpu-daemon, dcc-hard-limit-enforce \- CPU frequency daemon of Dudes Control Center
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dcc-cpu-daemon
.RB [ \-\-foreground | \-f ]
.br
.B dcc-hard-limit-enforce
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dcc-cpu-daemon
runs as root from
.BR dcc-cpu-daemon.service .
It sets the CPU governor and the minimum and maximum frequency of each core
through
.IR /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq ,
applies CPU profiles and keeps every core at or below the hard frequency
limit.
Profiles and the hard limit come from the desktop user's
.I profiles.json
(see
.BR dudes-control-center (7));
on start the daemon applies the hard limit to all cores.
.PP
On laptops that offer a firmware power profile
.RI ( /sys/firmware/acpi/platform_profile ,
for example low-power, balanced, performance), a frequency profile can set
one when it is applied (1.43.0; stored as
.B platform_profile
in the profile, empty = unchanged; set with
.BI SET_CUSTOM_PROFILE_PLATFORM| index | choice
or
.BR \- ).
STATUS reports
.B PLATFORM_PROFILE=
and
.BR PLATFORM_PROFILE_CHOICES= .
If power-profiles-daemon runs, it may change the profile as well.
.PP
Battery charge limit (1.46.0): where the first battery in
.I /sys/class/power_supply
offers
.BR charge_control_end_threshold ,
.BI SET_BATTERY_LIMIT| percent
(50 to 100, 100 = off) sets it and stores it in
.IR /etc/dudes-control-center/battery-limit .
The daemon sets the stored limit again at start and checks it every
minute, because many firmwares reset it after a reboot or standby.
A start threshold
.RB ( charge_control_start_threshold )
at or above the new limit is lowered to 10 below it.
STATUS reports
.BR BATTERY_LIMIT= " (\-1 if not offered),"
.B BATTERY_LIMIT_SAVED=
and
.BR BATTERY_LIMIT_DEVICE= .
.PP
The CPU temperature comes from coretemp, k10temp (Tdie where it exists) or
zenpower, otherwise from a CPU thermal zone.
.PP
Clients connect to
.IR /run/dcc-cpu.sock ;
only root and members of the group
.B dudes-control-center
are accepted.
.PP
.B dcc-hard-limit-enforce
is a fail-safe that works without the daemon:
.B dcc-hard-limit-enforcer.timer
runs it every 10 seconds (first 10 seconds after boot).
It reads the hard limit from
.I profiles.json
with the rights of that file's owner and lowers every core whose maximum
frequency is above it.
.SH NOTES
A maximum frequency is a ceiling, not a target: with the
.B intel_pstate
driver the CPU picks its own speed below it.
Many CPUs do not go below a certain speed once they have work to do, however
low the maximum is.
An Intel Core Ultra 9 275HX keeps about 2.2\~GHz on its P-cores and
1.5\~GHz on its E-cores, also at low load; maximums above that are kept
exactly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-foreground ", " \-f
Stay in the foreground (the service uses this).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/dcc-cpu.sock
Command socket.
.TP
.I /var/log/dudes-control-center/CPU_DAEMON.log
Daemon log (rotated at 1 MB, 5 old files kept).
.TP
.I /var/log/dudes-control-center/HARD_LIMIT_ENFORCER.log
Log of the fail-safe (capped at 1 MB).
.TP
.I /etc/dudes-control-center/battery-limit
The stored battery charge limit (missing = off).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/dcc-cpu-daemon.service
.TQ
.I /etc/systemd/system/dcc-hard-limit-enforcer.service
.TQ
.I /etc/systemd/system/dcc-hard-limit-enforcer.timer
Units; none of them starts while
.I /etc/dudes-control-center/disabled-cpu
exists.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DCC_PROFILES_PATH
Absolute path of the
.I profiles.json
to use, for example set in a systemd drop-in.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dudes-control-center (7),
.BR dcc-cpu-cli (1),
.BR dcc-gui (1),
.BR dcc-component (8),
.BR systemd.timer (5)
